.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH murk-agent-revoke 1  "revoke " 
.SH NAME
murk\-agent\-revoke \- Revoke an agent grant
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBmurk agent revoke\fR [\fB\-\-rotate\fR] [\fB\-\-values\-from\fR] [\fB\-\-vault\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fINAME\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Revoke an agent grant
.PP
The grant\*(Aqs key can still decrypt old versions of the vault from git history, so rotate the secrets it could read: pass `\-\-rotate`, or answer the prompt on a terminal.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-rotate\fR
Rotate the secrets the grant could read, in the same run. murk stores a new value for each. It does not rotate them at the provider
.TP
\fB\-\-values\-from\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
With \-\-rotate, read new values from a dotenv file of KEY=value lines (`\-` for stdin)
.TP
\fB\-\-vault\fR \fI<VAULT>\fR [default: .murk]
Vault filename
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
<\fINAME\fR>
Grant name
